PCIe BAR Read Benchmark Tool (Standalone Package)
=================================================

This is a complete out-of-tree solution for benchmarking PCIe BAR (Base Address
Register) space read performance. It includes a custom kernel module that provides
relaxed access to PCIe MMIO space, bypassing CONFIG_STRICT_DEVMEM restrictions.

Directory Structure
-------------------
pcibar_benchmark/
├── relaxed_devmem/          # Kernel module source
│   ├── Makefile
│   └── relaxed_devmem.c
├── pcibar_bench_relaxed.c   # Userspace benchmark tool
├── Makefile                  # Build userspace application
├── build.sh                  (Convenience build script)
└── README                    # This file

Building
--------
1. Build the kernel module:
   cd pcibar_benchmark/relaxed_devmem
   make

2. Build the userspace benchmark:
   cd pcibar_benchmark
   make

Or use the convenience script:
   cd pcibar_benchmark
   ./build.sh

Usage
-----
1. Load the kernel module:
   sudo insmod relaxed_devmem/relaxed_devmem.ko

2. Run the benchmark:
   sudo ./pcibar_bench_relaxed [options]

3. Unload the kernel module when done:
   sudo rmmod relaxed_devmem

Benchmark Options
----------------
  -d <domain>     PCI domain (default: 0)
  -b <bus>        PCI bus number (default: 0)
  -D <device>      PCI device number (default: 0)
  -f <function>    PCI function number (default: 0)
  -B <bar>         BAR number (default: 0)
  -i <iterations>  Number of iterations (default: 1000)
  -s <size>        Block size in bytes (default: 64)
  -n <bytes>       Bytes per iteration (default: BAR size)
  -r               Random access pattern
  -h               Print help message

Examples
--------
# Benchmark DMA engine device BAR2, reading 4KB per iteration
sudo ./pcibar_bench_relaxed -d 0x0 -b 0x7b -D 0x00 -f 0x0 -B 0x2 -i 100 -s 64 -n 4096

# Benchmark RDMA device BAR0, reading 1MB per iteration
sudo ./pcibar_bench_relaxed -d 0x0 -b 0x81 -D 0x00 -f 0x0 -B 0x0 -i 10 -s 256 -n 1048576

# Random access benchmark
sudo ./pcibar_bench_relaxed -d 0x0 -b 0x7b -D 0x00 -f 0x0 -B 0x2 -i 500 -s 64 -n 2048 -r

Finding PCI Devices
-----------------
# List all PCI devices
lspci

# Get detailed device information
lspci -vvv -s <bus:device.function>

# Check BAR information
cat /sys/bus/pci/devices/<domain:bus:device.function>/resource

Parameters Explained
------------------
- iterations: Number of times to repeat the read operation
- block_size: Size of each individual read operation (in bytes)
- bytes_per_iteration: Total bytes to read in each iteration
  * If not specified, defaults to reading the entire BAR space
  * Must not exceed the BAR size
  * Useful for limiting read size on large BAR spaces

Performance Metrics
------------------
- Total bytes read: Total amount of data read
- Elapsed time: Total benchmark execution time
- Throughput: Data transfer rate in MB/s
- Operations: Number of complete iterations per second

Requirements
------------
- Root privileges
- Kernel headers and build tools
- Target PCI device with BAR space
- ARM64 or x86_64 architecture

Security Considerations
---------------------
- The relaxed_devmem module bypasses CONFIG_STRICT_DEVMEM restrictions
- This allows access to physical memory that may be restricted
- Only use on trusted systems and for legitimate testing purposes
- Never run on production systems without proper authorization

Troubleshooting
---------------
1. Module load failure:
   - Check kernel logs: dmesg | tail
   - Ensure kernel headers match running kernel
   - Verify build tools are installed

2. Benchmark hangs:
   - Reduce bytes_per_iteration for large BAR spaces
   - Try smaller block sizes
   - Check device status with lspci

3. Permission denied:
   - Ensure running as root or with sudo
   - Check CAP_SYS_RAWIO capability

Cleaning
--------
# Clean kernel module build
cd relaxed_devmem
make clean

# Clean userspace application build
cd ..
make clean

# Clean everything
./build.sh clean

Notes
-----
- This is an out-of-tree module that doesn't require kernel source modifications
- Compatible with kernels that have CONFIG_STRICT_DEVMEM enabled
- The module creates /dev/relaxed_devmem for memory access
- All BAR reads are performed with non-cached memory mapping

